#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110291 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110670 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ac4127f-1253-5aee-b524-84e5c6b2bc64" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Angles From Right, Straight, and Full Angles Using Algebra"}] :question/id #uuid "60df6f7a-5257-5081-ba0c-0e9499a15f23" :question/math-academy-id "e-10588" :question/problem "![](images/44/44aa56708e43f0518d7759e96b0770834f999998280c52910b2ca07cd607c1ab.png)\n\nGiven that $\\angle DOA$ is a straight angle, what is the value of $x$?" :question/worked-solution "Note that $\\angle AOD$ is a straight angle and $\\angle BOC$ is a right angle. Hence,\n\n$$\nm∠AOD = 180^{∘}, m∠BOC = 90^{∘}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠AOB + m∠BOC + m∠COD &= m∠AOD \\\\\nx + 90^{∘} + (2x - 30^{∘}) &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n3x + 60^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n3x &= 120^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 40^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110387 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110759 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d62f753-a3e0-5c7e-919d-3f878c1d8e93" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Measure of Unknown Angles on a Diagram"}] :question/id #uuid "826ef015-53ba-5889-8749-170a656c35f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-2590" :question/problem "In the figure shown below, determine the measure of $\\angle EOA$.\n\n![](images/80/80f47745a289bf021a4566a58d1961c93bb216e2df900b8effae8f2dda3f1606.png)" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, $\\angle COD$ is a right angle, so\n\n$$\nm\\angle COD = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\nAlso, since $\\angle COD$ and $\\angle EOB$ are vertical angles, they have the same measure:\n\n$$\nm\\angle EOB=m\\angle COD=90^\\circ\n$$\n\nNow, since we know that\n\n$$\nm\\angle AOB=40^{\\circ}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nm\\angle EOB = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\nwe can use the angle addition postulate to solve for $m\\angle EOA$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠EOA + m∠AOB &= m∠EOB \\\\\nm∠EOA + 40^{∘} &= 90^{∘} \\\\\nm∠EOA &= 50^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110390 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110760 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f64be210-e966-5be7-b7b4-cbf859793e76" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Angle Types From a Diagram"}] :question/id #uuid "6438b232-f40a-54b0-a599-a1d6aa4151d2" :question/math-academy-id "e-2599" :question/problem "![](images/80/800f11f555f0c2be0e89aa26602cfe4a35a62f4fe2309dfe2a188f47138fb98b.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above. Which of the following statements is true?\n\n1. $\\angle A$ is obtuse\n2. $\\angle B$ is a right angle\n3. $\\angle A$ and $\\angle B$ are vertical angles" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the diagram, $\\angle A$ and the angle with measure $130^\\circ$ are vertical angles. Therefore,\n$m\\angle A = 130^\\circ > 90^\\circ$.\nConsequently, $\\angle A$ is an obtuse angle.\n- Statement II is false. From the diagram, $\\angle B$ and the angle with measure $50^\\circ$ are vertical angles. Therefore,\n$m\\angle B = 50^\\circ \\neq 90^\\circ$.\nConsequently, $\\angle B$ is not a right angle.\n- Statement III is false. Angles $\\angle A$ and $\\angle B$ are not vertical since they have a common side.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110391 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110761 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fea607a9-7512-5062-b4ae-c53e0f653efd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Angle Type From a Description"}] :question/id #uuid "15470df7-8d18-5427-9488-f3f10df13952" :question/math-academy-id "e-2601" :question/problem "Let $\\angle S$ and $\\angle T$ be two adjacent angles. Given that $\\angle S$ is a straight angle and $\\angle T$ is right, what type of angle is their sum?" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\angle S$ is a straight angle, we have\n\n$$\nm\\angle S=180^\\circ\n$$\n\nand since $\\angle T$ is a right angle, we have\n\n$$\nm\\angle T=90^{\\circ}\n$$\n\nSo, their sum is\n\n$$\nm\\angle S + m\\angle T=180^{\\circ} + 90^{\\circ}=270^{\\circ}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sum of $\\angle S$ and $\\angle T$ is a reflex angle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111246 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110759 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d62f753-a3e0-5c7e-919d-3f878c1d8e93" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Measure of Unknown Angles on a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87c77a89-5776-5dc6-a5b0-fe856399828d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109202} {:answer/id #uuid "e4e16d6a-c2e1-5637-bf5b-a768382e3df0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109203} {:answer/id #uuid "14cbb427-155d-5397-bacc-8c6e0c671628" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109204} {:answer/id #uuid "5b177d9d-1079-578b-b8e1-78e07a5638d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "55^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109205} {:answer/id #uuid "6f5f0826-8a46-543c-b81e-4e970604b7ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109206}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2f6cc1ff-d18a-5058-b987-d452bdea6e0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109201}] :question/id #uuid "c27c7256-8071-50ae-b777-2aae5eb73a2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-52734" :question/problem "![](images/f7/f74bf7ac817f0e1612f09728f5198578e453dc7799d74402c7826d1416d86845.png)\n\nIn the figure shown above, determine the measure of $∠GOH$ given that $m∠BOD = 120^{∘}$."}]}
